            title: "Old Norse",
            description: "The mother tongue of the Viking Age and its Scandinavian evolution.",
            lore: "<p>Old Norse, the language of the Vikings, is no longer spoken today. However, it evolved to give rise to the modern Scandinavian languages, of which Icelandic and Faroese are the closest and most faithful descendants.</p><p>The Viking languages are divided into two main branches today:</p><h4 class='codex-section-title'>1. The Western Branch</h4><ul><li><strong>Icelandic:</strong> It is the modern language closest to Old Norse. Due to its isolation, it has evolved very little, allowing speakers to read sagas that are nearly a thousand years old with almost no difficulty.</li><li><strong>Faroese:</strong> Spoken in the Faroe Islands, this language is very similar to Icelandic and shares the same Old West Norse roots.</li></ul><h4 class='codex-section-title'>2. The Eastern Branch</h4><ul><li><strong>Norwegian:</strong> Divided into two official written forms (Bokmål and Nynorsk).</li><li><strong>Swedish and Danish:</strong> These languages have diverged more over the centuries, simplifying their grammar compared to Old Norse, while remaining highly mutually intelligible with Norwegian.</li></ul><h4 class='codex-section-title'>Linguistic Heritage</h4><p>Although Old Norse is an extinct language, its influence survives in many English words (like sky or window) and in thousands of place names in Europe, particularly in the British Isles and Normandy.</p>",
